  "Name": "Baumeister1967-Fig9-Kard-FP",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table II (p. 900); Fig. 9 caption and text pp. 900-901",
  "Notes": "Kard's modified Fabry-Perot tunnel layer filter: four extra n_l = 1.55 layers make the s and p passbands coincide at 60 deg\nSpecification: p and s passbands should coincide (polarization-independent FTR filter).\nReported: Fig. 9 (incident angle 60 deg, n_l = 1.550): main p and s passbands nearly coincide near 0.61 um with peak T ~1 (p slightly narrower); small spikes near 0.42-0.46 um and ~0.63 um (plot). Text p. 901: with n_l = 1.545 the s and p transmission bands would exactly overlap.\nNotes: Table II: thickness 'in quarter-wave optical thickness' except layers 2 and 6 in geometrical thickness; the footnote gives no unit, but by the convention of Tables I and III (QWOT 'in microns') the values are the wavelengths in um at which each layer is a quarter wave: layers 1,3,5,7 = 2.473 (n 1.55) -> 1 QWOT at reference_nm 2473 (d = 398.871 nm); layer 4 = 4.116 (n 1.72) -> d = 4116/(4*1.72) = 598.25581 nm; layers 2 and 6 = 0.308 um physical, n 1.38. Incident and emergent medium 1.72 (footnote), angle 60 deg inside it (Fig. 9 caption). Own TMM check supports this reading: s peak 0.6158 um and p peak 0.6148 um, T ~1; with n_l = 1.545 both peak at 0.6016 um (exact overlap, as the text says). Layer 4 index 1.720 equals the media index; it is a separate material only because layers must be named. Symmetric design.\nLayers from Table II (p. 900); Fig. 9 caption and text pp. 900-901; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours p peak at 614.9 nm, s peak at 615.8 nm; paper s and p bands nearly coincide near 610 nm (Fig. 9).",
